{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1969-twoM-MDM-halfwave",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Radiant power flow and absorptance in thin films,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 423-436 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.000423 - Fig. 21 caption, p. 433: air (LH)^10 1.828L M 0.5D HH 0.5D M 1.828L (HL)^8 quartz",
  "Notes": "Two-M UV bandpass filter with a half-wave H layer in the centre of the cryolite spacer to suppress the long-wave leak\nSpecification: Same as the two-M filter, with absorbing stack M 0.5D HH 0.5D M.\nReported: p. 433, Fig. 21 (log T, 0.2-0.4 um; dashed = this design, solid = Fig. 20 curve): peak ~0.5 near 0.255 um; transmittance outside the passband considerably lower than Fig. 20, the 0.29 um leak drops to ~1e-2. Check computed here (nondispersive): peak T = 0.537 at 255.2 nm, T = 0.014 at 290 nm, ~3e-4 at 220-240 nm.\nNotes: Normal incidence assumed (no angle given). The notation is read left to right from the incident medium (air) to the substrate (quartz, n = 1.47), so layer 1 is next to air. All dielectric layers are quarter-wave optical thickness (QWOT) at lambda0 = 2536 A = 253.6 nm with constant indices; the Al film is a physical thickness with the nondispersive n - jk = 0.20 - j2.80 at 2536 A (Macleod sign convention; k = +2.80 here). Symbols as in Fig. 19 (L, D cryolite 1.36; H index 1.55; D = QWOT at 3900 A). HH (two adjacent H quarter waves) is entered as one 2-QWOT layer. Expanded: (LH)^10 (20), 1.828L, M, 0.5D, 2H, 0.5D, M, 1.828L, (HL)^8 (16): 43 layers (44 if HH is counted as two).\nLayers from Fig. 21 caption, p. 433: air (LH)^10 1.828L M 0.5D HH 0.5D M 1.828L (HL)^8 quartz; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours peak T 0.537 at 255.2 nm; T 0.014 at 290 nm; paper ~0.5 near 255 nm, leak at 290 nm down to ~1e-2 (Fig. 21).",
